{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 21, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful ruler who reigned as Count of Tripoli from 1146 to 1193. He is a significant figure in the history of the Crusades and the Levant. His reign marked a period of significant expansion and consolidation of Tripoli's territory and influence. Raymond III inherited the countship at a young age and quickly proved to be a capable and ambitious leader. He skillfully navigated the complex political landscape of the Crusader states and the Muslim world.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension*\n\nRaymond III was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli. He was educated in the ways of governance military strategy and diplomacy. His early years were spent learning the intricacies of ruling a frontier territory constantly threatened by both Muslim and internal enemies. He ascended to the countship upon his father's death in 1146. Immediately he faced challenges including ongoing conflicts with the Zengi dynasty in Mosul and the Almohad dynasty in Egypt. He established a strong military presence and implemented policies aimed at strengthening Tripoli's defenses. He also focused on economic development and fostered trade relations with various European powers.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts*\n\nRaymond III's reign was characterized by numerous military campaigns. He engaged in several wars against the Zengi dynasty in northern Syria often with mixed success. His forces faced significant challenges due to the Zengi's superior numbers and fortifications. He also fought against the Almohad Caliphate in Egypt. Raymond III was known for his strategic acumen and his willingness to engage in siege warfare. He led expeditions to secure control of important coastal cities and fortified positions. These campaigns expanded Tripoli's territory and solidified its position as a major power in the Levant. He also maintained alliances with other Crusader states like Antioch and Jerusalem to counter common enemies.\n\n*Legacy and End of Reign*\n\nRaymond III's reign is considered a golden age for Tripoli. He oversaw a period of significant prosperity and expansion. He fostered trade and agriculture and built numerous fortifications. His military successes secured Tripoli's borders and its independence. However his reign also saw increasing tensions with the Muslim world. In 1193 Raymond III died in battle against Saladin the Ayyubid sultan. He was succeeded by his son Raymond IV. Raymond III's legacy as a skilled military leader a shrewd diplomat and a successful ruler continues to be recognized by historians. He is remembered as one of the most important counts of Tripoli and a key figure in the Crusader history of the Levant.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.1805190145969391}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -5.98412734689191e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.08811502158641815}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.031204013153910637}, {"token": "▁powerful", "logprob": -0.5400348901748657}, {"token": "▁ruler", "logprob": -0.6650604605674744}, {"token": "▁who", "logprob": -0.6149573922157288}, {"token": "▁reigned", "logprob": -0.5698574185371399}, {"token": "▁as", "logprob": -0.9908742308616638}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -0.0009411911014467478}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -0.0009457168052904308}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": -5.07818695041351e-05}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.0011162485461682081}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.002866566414013505}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00011884459672728553}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.0010288427583873272}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.7527305483818054}, {"token": "6", "logprob": -0.08973618596792221}, {"token": "▁to", "logprob": -0.1801406741142273}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.00057859369